# core/view-contents - Which elements are placed on this diagram?
#
# Answers: the elements a view depicts, with label, native id and every type each
# carries. The inverse of core/view-usage, which asks which diagrams show one
# element.
#
# Does NOT prove: that the diagram is current, complete, or the authoritative
# picture of anything. What a view contains is an editorial choice by whoever drew
# it, so "not on the diagram" is not "not in the architecture" - and an element
# placed on it may have been left behind by a change nobody redrew.
#
# REQUIRES the views graph, emitted only where the source carried diagrams.
# Backstage and LeanIX output has no views graph at all, so under a profile where
# views_graph is partial an empty result may mean "this notation has no diagrams"
# rather than "this view is empty".
#
# The direction that catches people: the view resource lives in the SEMANTIC graph
# while the nodes that place elements on it live in the VIEWS graph. This template
# reaches across both, matching nodes by their view reference. Querying the view
# and its nodes in one graph returns nothing, silently.
#
# Nodes are matched on the view reference rather than the node class, because a
# node is typed archvis:ArchNode only when it is backed by an element; layout-only
# nodes are plain archvis:Node, and older converter output types every node that
# way. A node placing no element - a group box, a note - contributes no row here.
#
# Types are concatenated rather than given a row each. Elements are multi-typed, a
# core class plus a notation class, so one row per type would triple the result and
# read as three elements. To narrow to a single type instead, use
# core/elements-by-type; to see what kinds of thing a diagram mixes, read the
# types column.
#
# STR() around ?type is load-bearing, not tidying. GROUP_CONCAT over IRIs is a type
# error, and an erroring aggregate yields an unbound result rather than a failure -
# so without it every row arrives with an empty types column while the row count
# still looks right.
#
# Get VIEW_IRI from core/views rather than constructing it.
#
# Parameters: VIEW_IRI, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?element ?label (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT STR(?type); separator=", ") AS ?types) ?nativeId
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:views}}
    VALUES ?view { {{VIEW_IRI}} }
    ?node {{ROLE:view_ref}} ?view ;
          {{ROLE:node_element}} ?element .
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  OPTIONAL {
    {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
      ?element a ?type .
      OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:label}} ?label }
      OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:native_id}} ?nativeId }
    {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
  }
}
GROUP BY ?element ?label ?nativeId
ORDER BY ?label ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
